1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,650
How Glaciers Shape Landscapes.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:12,337
Glaciers are among the most powerful geological agents on Earth, capable of reshaping entire mountain ranges over timescales of thousands to millions of years.

3
00:00:12,337 --> 00:00:24,237
These slow-moving rivers of ice, which currently cover approximately 10 percent of Earth's land surface, erode, transport, and deposit rock on a scale unmatched by any other surface process.

4
00:00:24,237 --> 00:00:28,150
Glacial erosion operates through two primary mechanisms.

5
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:36,187
The first, abrasion, occurs when rock fragments embedded in the base of a glacier scrape against the underlying bedrock like sandpaper.

6
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:45,950
As the glacier moves—typically 10 to 300 meters per year for temperate glaciers—these embedded particles grind the bedrock into fine-grained rock flour.

7
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:56,950
Measurements from the Bench Glacier in Alaska show that abrasion rates can reach 5 millimeters per year on soft bedrock, a rate that over 10,000 years would remove 50 meters of rock.

8
00:00:56,950 --> 00:01:05,362
The second mechanism, plucking or quarrying, happens when meltwater penetrates cracks in the bedrock, freezes, and expands.

9
00:01:05,362 --> 00:01:10,250
The ice then pulls away loosened blocks of rock, some the size of houses.

10
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:17,662
Plucking is most effective on the downstream side of bedrock obstacles, where pressure differences cause meltwater to refreeze.

11
00:01:17,662 --> 00:01:22,050
Together, abrasion and plucking produce distinctive landforms.

12
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:30,462
U-shaped valleys, with their characteristic steep walls and flat floors, result from glacial widening of pre-existing river valleys.

13
00:01:30,462 --> 00:01:42,175
Yosemite Valley in California, carved by repeated glaciations over 2 million years, descends from 1,200-meter cliffs to a flat valley floor 900 meters below.

14
00:01:42,175 --> 00:01:50,275
Cirques, armchair-shaped depressions at mountain headwalls, form where ice accumulates and rotates in place, deepening the bedrock.

15
00:01:50,275 --> 00:01:59,662
When two cirques erode toward each other from opposite sides of a ridge, they create a sharp, knife-edged arête—the Matterhorn's iconic ridge is a classic example.

16
00:01:59,662 --> 00:02:03,900
As glaciers retreat, they leave behind depositional features.

17
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:12,837
Till, an unsorted mixture of clay, sand, and boulders, accumulates as lateral and terminal moraines that mark a glacier's former extent.

18
00:02:12,837 --> 00:02:24,137
Much of the fertile agricultural soil of the North American Midwest, deposited 10,000 to 15,000 years ago during the Laurentide Ice Sheet's retreat, originated as glacial till.

19
00:02:24,137 --> 00:02:36,250
Today, 90 percent of the world's glaciers are retreating due to rising global temperatures, a trend documented by the World Glacier Monitoring Service across 5,000 reference glaciers since 1850.

